< *Added later on 28 November.* **With many marks, nearly every way of choosing the signs is good.** On paper it is now proved that on every clock, for any m marks, the bad ways of choosing the signs 
< are at most 3.12 divided by √m of all the ways (√m is the square root of the number of marks), and fewer than 2 divided by √m of them when the number of marks is odd. So with 100 marks at least 
< 68 ways in 100 are good, and with 10,000 marks at least 96 in 100; with 101 marks more than 80 in 100, and with 10,001 more than 98 in 100. For few marks the half and the quarter of the paragraphs 
< above say more: the new bound says more than the half from 17 marks on, for odd numbers of marks, and more than the quarter from 20 marks on, for even numbers. The rate cannot be bettered: if all the 
< marks are on one hour, the bad ways are those with as many minuses as pluses, which for an even number of marks are about 0.8 divided by √m of all the ways.
