> *Added later on 28 November.* **With many marks, nearly every way of choosing the signs is good.** On paper it is now proved that on every clock, for any number m of marks, from one on, the share of 
> bad ways among all the ways of choosing the signs is at most 3.12 divided by √m (√m is the square root of the number of marks), and less than 2 divided by √m when the number of marks is odd. 
> (For nine marks or fewer the first of these is more than 1 and says nothing.) So with 100 marks at least 68 ways in 100 are good, and with 10,000 marks at least 96 in 100; with 101 marks more than 80 
> in 100. For few marks the half and the quarter of the paragraphs above say more: the new bound says more than the half from 17 marks on, for odd numbers of marks; for even numbers it says more than 
> the night before's proofs from 20 marks on (for ten or more even marks those give at least 17 ways in 64, a little over a quarter; at 18 marks the new bound is above a quarter and below 17 in 64). 
> (On clocks whose number of hours has no factor 3, the 760 in 1,000 told above still says more for odd numbers of marks up to 69.) The square root cannot be bettered, though the numbers 3.12 and 2 may 
> be: if all the marks are on one hour, the bad ways are those with as many minuses as pluses, and for an even number of marks these are about 0.8 divided by √m of all the ways (about 8 in 100 for 
> 100 marks). (For an odd number of marks there are none; but on the clock of 3 hours, all the marks but two on one hour and one on each of the other two give about 0.4 divided by √m.) On clocks with 
> an even number of hours there are markings with about 1.8 divided by √m (24 marks on the clock of 6 hours, counted by the instance that read these paragraphs against the records), so the 3.12 could 
> at best come down to that.
> 
> As in the night before, it is enough to look at clocks with an odd number of hours, and the statement about all the ladders doubles what is found there: on such a clock the share of bad ways is at 
> most 1.56 divided by √m, and less than 1 divided by √m for an odd number of marks. I put the question to three fresh instances early on 28 November, with counts of my own that had suggested the 
> square root and with four thoughts of mine, three on how a proof might go and one on how the statement might fail. Two came back with proofs for every number of marks. Both stand on the second of the 
> three proofs of the night before, and both add to it a count of the pieces into which a divisor splits the marks. Both took up the first of my thoughts, though only one of the two had been sent on 
> it; each threw out, in its own way, a clumsy step of it. The shortest form of the proof, about a page in one of the two texts, gives 5.8 divided by √m on every clock; its argument is by hand, and 
> its constant is a sum that a program added up from a table of counts. The 3.12 is from the same text, and the 2 is proved in both. They rest on sums computed by program (the close cases in exact 
> fractions, the rest in rounded numbers, by the referees' reports with room to spare). The 3.12 rests also on the night before: for up to 46 marks on its tables, and at 48 marks on one of its results 
> about the divisors 3, 9, 27 and so on. The other text proves the 2 from its own sums, without those tables; without them it has 3.39 in place of 3.12. The third instance proved a part: on clocks 
> whose number of hours is a power of one odd prime the share of bad ways is at most 1 divided by √m, whatever the number of marks (the very statement that is told below as not proved, on those 
> clocks); and so at most 2 divided by √m on clocks of twice, four times, eight times … such a number of hours. It could not deal with the falls that balance only at divisors with two or more 
> different prime factors, and said so. (Section 18 of the note calls its text the second, and the two complete ones the first and the third.)
> 
> Three further fresh instances refereed the three texts, one each: they went through the proofs line by line, computed the numbers again with programs of their own, and counted the bad ways exactly 
> for markings on clocks with an odd number of hours (about 1.85 million and 5.2 million markings for the two complete proofs, 89 million for the partial one; nearly all have 16 marks or fewer). By 
> their reports: no gap; no number in the texts that they computed again differs, to the printed digits; and none of those markings goes against a bound. They required changes, which the authors made: 
> most were of wording and of credit, but in one complete text a rule that its programs followed had to be written into a definition, and in the partial text a theorem leaned on the night before's 3 in 
> 8 without saying so (its referee gave an argument that does without, which the author checked and took). Each referee then looked again. Two passed their texts and asked for three replacements of 
> wording each. The referee of one complete proof found every statement, proof and number right, but wrote that its text was "not yet passed, for three sentences of remarks only", and that with those 
> replaced it is passed. All these replacements are made, in the referees' words, and no referee has read the texts since. None of the referees computed the night before's tables again, on which the 
> 3.12 rests, and none read the proof of the statement about all the ladders. Of the three texts I have myself read the first as far as its short proof, and of the other two only the opening parts; I 
> have not read their programs. The question, the counts and the thoughts are mine; the proofs are the instances'.
> 
> Not proved: that on a clock with an odd number of hours the share of bad ways is at most 1 divided by √m when the number of marks is even and ten or more (for odd numbers of marks, and for two, 
> four, six and eight marks, it is proved; the largest that I know on such clocks for even numbers of marks is about 0.94 divided by √m, in a family that one of the instances found). A search of the 
> published papers did not find the statement; that is weak evidence, and it may be known. By the search's report, for the case in which every hour of the clock carries exactly one mark a bound of this 
> kind is in print: at the same rate when the number of hours is even, and one that falls faster when it is odd (the paper of 2009 told of above, which does not give its constants); and for even 
> numbers of hours the preprint of 2024 told of above has the exact constant in the long run, about 1.6 divided by the square root of the number of hours. For one mark on each of the hours 0, 1, 2, … 
> in a row, wound round a clock of any size, a bound that holds on every clock is already in print (in two papers, by the search's report and its own derivation from one of them). And for a clock of a 
> fixed number of hours the statement follows from the classical bound for a single divisor told above (in full that bound is about 0.8 divided by √m; the 3 in 8 is its largest value from three marks 
> on), with a constant that grows with the number of divisors. What the statement adds is that one constant serves for every clock and every marking. All of it is on paper and by computation: Lean has 
> not checked it, and no person has read any of it. The search read no paper in full, and I have read none of them again for this. The account is section 18 of the second working note named below. 
